[SPEAKER_00]: Outer world, up to certain level, there is nowhere to go.

175
00:23:42,486 --> 00:23:46,247
[SPEAKER_00]: But inner development, there is so far we can go.

176
00:23:47,736 --> 00:24:01,839
[SPEAKER_00]: So by cultivating compassion, loving kindness, developing inner mindfulness and all those things, way to give meaning to one's life.

177
00:24:02,539 --> 00:24:16,002
[SPEAKER_00]: So with this kind of attitude, so these are the attitudes or the proper attitude we call it, to have, to do meditation or practice.

178
00:24:18,215 --> 00:24:35,441
[SPEAKER_00]: So then when you meditate, there are some different traditions, different methods, there are some different methods, but in our particular tradition we have called seven posture.

179
00:24:37,041 --> 00:25:00,191
[SPEAKER_00]: which correspond with the right posture correspond with right channel with right channel correspond with flow of air we call it air so and then with proper posture and proper what you say proper channel then I think we can have

180
00:25:03,132 --> 00:25:09,115
[SPEAKER_00]: better or more clear understanding of mind or control over mind.

181
00:25:13,757 --> 00:25:22,882
[SPEAKER_00]: In some teachings it is said that there are lots of arhats meditating in the forest and some monkeys they like to imitate.

182
00:25:24,362 --> 00:25:31,886
[SPEAKER_00]: So when the arhats are meditating and monkeys are also meditating like this and they said they also achieve some kind of inner bliss.

183
00:25:32,530 --> 00:25:33,751
[SPEAKER_00]: even though they couldn't think much.

184
00:25:34,411 --> 00:25:39,894
[SPEAKER_00]: So it shows how important even outer posture is, correspond with our inner meditation.

185
00:25:41,955 --> 00:25:44,316
[SPEAKER_00]: So there's what we call seven posture.

186
00:25:44,577 --> 00:25:55,983
[SPEAKER_00]: The number one is to have a straight spine, sometimes given like an arrow or sometimes given like the coins pile up, spine straight.

187
00:26:01,097 --> 00:26:03,178
[SPEAKER_00]: Cross leg or lotus or vajra leg.

188
00:26:04,038 --> 00:26:06,779
[SPEAKER_00]: The lotus posture is a little bit like what we are sitting now.

189
00:26:09,260 --> 00:26:15,862
[SPEAKER_00]: And if vajra posture is too not comfortable, I think normally we recommend this kind of posture.

190
00:26:16,503 --> 00:26:17,023
[SPEAKER_00]: Cross leg.

191
00:26:19,043 --> 00:26:25,786
[SPEAKER_00]: And then eyes not up, not right, left, but sort of gazing a little bit down towards your nose.

192
00:26:26,746 --> 00:26:28,687
[SPEAKER_00]: Natarpab, sort of level of your nose.

193
00:26:32,563 --> 00:26:37,609
[SPEAKER_00]: then putting your hands sort of falls together like the Amitabha Buddha.

194
00:26:38,770 --> 00:26:40,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Economical Mudra we call this.

195
00:26:42,373 --> 00:26:46,637
[SPEAKER_00]: and then tongue sort of putting up, what you call?

196
00:26:46,698 --> 00:26:47,158
[SPEAKER_00]: Palate.

197
00:26:48,279 --> 00:26:49,741
[SPEAKER_00]: Palate, tongue up like this.

198
00:26:50,942 --> 00:26:51,322
[SPEAKER_00]: Tongue up.

199
00:26:53,004 --> 00:26:56,087
[SPEAKER_00]: And then putting a shoulder a little bit like this.

200
00:26:56,948 --> 00:27:03,394
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes we say a little bit like an eagle's posture or a little bit like that.

201
00:27:04,835 --> 00:27:06,057
[SPEAKER_00]: Vultures, sorry, vultures.

202
00:27:07,778 --> 00:27:12,802
[SPEAKER_00]: And then head a little bit bent down like that so it's easy to breathe.

203
00:27:12,862 --> 00:27:17,486
[SPEAKER_00]: So this kind of posture is called the seven postures.

204
00:27:18,767 --> 00:27:19,808
[SPEAKER_00]: Seven point postures.

205
00:27:35,413 --> 00:27:39,315
[SPEAKER_00]: So then we go into meditation.

206
00:27:39,375 --> 00:27:41,976
[SPEAKER_00]: I think I will ask Matthew to take over.

207
00:27:47,359 --> 00:28:00,446
[SPEAKER_01]: So actually before doing the meditation, say a few words about what we will meditate upon to give you the background.

208
00:28:02,225 --> 00:28:17,157
[SPEAKER_01]: So once, as Tempoche mentioned, we have changed a little bit our mind and attitude to appreciate the extraordinary opportunity we have to engage in the practice.

209
00:28:18,578 --> 00:28:30,327
[SPEAKER_01]: But this time now, we have this human life, we're still alive, we're not too much subject to impermanence so far, it hasn't hit us too hard, and we

210
00:28:30,909 --> 00:28:57,682
[SPEAKER_01]: get the right karmic condition that we could meet the dharma and then we feel some kind of lassitude towards the worldly preoccupation and these endless circles and that like ripples on the surface of the ocean that never end and our teacher Khyentse Rinpoche said as far as the worldly activities are concerned

211
00:28:59,828 --> 00:29:25,879
[SPEAKER_01]: the idea that it could one day we could finally complete all of them is completely an illusion it's never any end to those that the waves on the ocean the only way is to sometime at some point decide to drop them otherwise they will never stop by themselves and so

212
00:29:27,542 --> 00:29:43,731
[SPEAKER_01]: having to somehow disengage our mind from those preoccupations and focus it on the Dharma, then we should realize that, yes, we want to get rid of suffering, but what's the point if we just think of ourself?

213
00:29:44,771 --> 00:29:53,976
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a bit like all our friends, our family, and we are all together prisoners in a terrible sort of labor camp.

214
00:29:55,577 --> 00:29:57,238
[SPEAKER_01]: And then we sort of manage to escape.

215
00:29:58,550 --> 00:30:27,725
[SPEAKER_01]: we find a way to escape but will we feel comfortable if we escape all alone leaving everyone who are dear to us behind so obviously once we find there is a way to escape and then our first thought is how could we all escape together or should be so that's the motivation of the great vehicle there are also different levels

216
00:30:30,176 --> 00:30:33,999
[SPEAKER_01]: depending on how much concerned we are with others.

217
00:30:35,279 --> 00:30:38,542
[SPEAKER_01]: There are three main examples.

218
00:30:38,622 --> 00:30:40,563
[SPEAKER_01]: Sort of the lesser one is like the king.

219
00:30:41,864 --> 00:30:47,108
[SPEAKER_01]: The king first establishes his kingdom, makes everything safe, and then he takes care of his subjects.

220
00:30:47,188 --> 00:30:54,153
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's like the person who wants first to achieve liberation from samsara, then takes care of the others.

221
00:30:55,094 --> 00:30:57,936
[SPEAKER_01]: The second one, which is a higher motivation,

222
00:30:58,774 --> 00:31:00,074
[SPEAKER_01]: is like the ferryman.

223
00:31:00,795 --> 00:31:09,098
[SPEAKER_01]: The ferryman and the person he ferries across the river or the ocean, they all cross together.

224
00:31:10,258 --> 00:31:20,701
[SPEAKER_01]: But they said the best or the most dedicated type of altruistic mind is like a shepherd.

225
00:31:21,642 --> 00:31:27,184
[SPEAKER_01]: The shepherd will be the last to enter the coral or the place where the sheep

226
00:31:28,994 --> 00:31:52,672
[SPEAKER_01]: kept in safety and until the last one is in complete safety and then he will be looking after them and enter the last so this is the kind of courage and determination that we should have now in order to be able to finally generate

227
00:31:54,090 --> 00:32:19,337
[SPEAKER_01]: what we call the Bodhicitta, that is the wish to achieve enlightenment for the sake of all sentient beings, we need to prepare our mind through cultivating in a limitless way four fundamental qualities which are attuned to compassion and altruistic mind.

228
00:32:21,237 --> 00:32:23,338
[SPEAKER_01]: And traditionally we call the four boundless

229
00:32:24,368 --> 00:32:30,970
[SPEAKER_01]: They are boundless because they apply to boundless sentient beings, infinite number of sentient beings.

230
00:32:31,830 --> 00:32:34,491
[SPEAKER_01]: And therefore, the need for it is boundless.

231
00:32:34,711 --> 00:32:38,212
[SPEAKER_01]: The merit accrued from having this thought is boundless.

232
00:32:41,393 --> 00:32:53,837
[SPEAKER_01]: And so these are mainly boundless loving-kindness, boundless compassion, boundless rejoicing, and boundless impartiality.

233
00:32:55,026 --> 00:32:57,268
[SPEAKER_01]: so what are they?

234
00:32:58,069 --> 00:33:20,087
[SPEAKER_01]: boundless loving-kindness is the root of all of them it's the unconditional benevolence and it's defined as the wish that may all sentient beings without any exception achieve happiness and the cause of happiness now

235
00:33:21,785 --> 00:33:25,106
[SPEAKER_01]: That is why we need to make it boundless.

236
00:33:25,526 --> 00:33:29,148
[SPEAKER_01]: People might say, oh, this is really unrealistic.

237
00:33:29,228 --> 00:33:30,308
[SPEAKER_01]: It's too difficult.

238
00:33:31,849 --> 00:33:38,011
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't make even sense to decide that we want to bring happiness to the infinity of sentient beings.

239
00:33:38,051 --> 00:33:40,312
[SPEAKER_01]: Why don't we just start at least by our neighbors?

240
00:33:41,332 --> 00:33:49,235
[SPEAKER_01]: And some people say, sometimes rightly so, it's easier to pray for all beings, but it's more difficult to take care of your neighbor and those you meet every day.

241
00:33:51,014 --> 00:33:54,615
[SPEAKER_01]: Because those are real people that you encounter, and it's more challenging.

242
00:33:55,255 --> 00:33:57,516
[SPEAKER_01]: It's easy to say, oh, may all sentient beings be happy.

243
00:33:58,396 --> 00:33:59,957
[SPEAKER_01]: And usually you think, except my neighbor.

244
00:34:01,717 --> 00:34:05,838
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the idea, why we start so big?

245
00:34:06,279 --> 00:34:10,860
[SPEAKER_01]: You could say, well, let me start with two people, and then I will see if I can make it, and I will increase.

246
00:34:12,220 --> 00:34:17,302
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the reason is that if we set the limit from the start, it's like any journey.

247
00:34:18,281 --> 00:34:21,562
[SPEAKER_01]: then you probably get to there, but not much farther away.

248
00:34:22,323 --> 00:34:29,126
[SPEAKER_01]: If you say, my only ambition in life is to get to Changi Airport, well, you'll get there.

249
00:34:29,646 --> 00:34:30,226
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's all.

250
00:34:31,447 --> 00:34:36,429
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, if you say, I want to go to Tahiti, well, if you manage to get there, that's great.

251
00:34:36,489 --> 00:34:39,090
[SPEAKER_01]: And on the way, you will get to Changi Airport for sure.

252
00:34:39,710 --> 00:34:41,271
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the first step will be included.

253
00:34:42,411 --> 00:34:43,151
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you...

254
00:34:43,812 --> 00:34:46,793
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no reason from the start to already...

255
00:34:47,805 --> 00:35:17,343
[SPEAKER_01]: limit yourself concede sort of defeat that you will never be able to help all sentient beings so it's better to go for the maximum number and then it would not make sense to say just hundred and it would not make sense even to say hundred million why stop there and also it certainly doesn't make sense to say all sentient beings except one hundred that I cannot stand so there's precisely those that you need to include otherwise

256
00:35:17,937 --> 00:35:33,906
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still mixed with a deluded mind that clings to the notion of friend, enemy as being intrinsic qualities of a sentient being.

257
00:35:35,607 --> 00:35:41,610
[SPEAKER_01]: So in that sense, it should be independent on the way the beings behave.

258
00:35:43,451 --> 00:35:47,233
[SPEAKER_01]: Loving kindness and compassion are not reward for good behavior.

259
00:35:48,635 --> 00:36:17,988
[SPEAKER_01]: the withdrawal of loving kindness and compassion cannot be a punishment for bad behavior morality and karma is something else it will take care of that but your compassion should be for your wish of happiness should be for any kind of happiness and likewise the second one compassion that is the wish that may all beings may be free from suffering so it is love applied to suffering it is

260
00:36:18,448 --> 00:36:31,675
[SPEAKER_01]: the compassion for any kind of suffering, wherever it is, whatever it is, and not just the suffering of those who are dear to me, or whom I know, and not care about strangers, and certainly not about enemies.

261
00:36:32,495 --> 00:36:43,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you think about it, since it's not a moral judgment, but it's getting at the root of suffering, even the worst dictator, the compassion is not wishing him good,

262
00:36:44,948 --> 00:37:06,447
[SPEAKER_01]: wishing that you have a nice holiday somewhere in the Adamant Islands or somewhere, but simply to wish may the hatred, the indifference, the cruelty that had made that person behave in such way, which are the causes of suffering for countless beings and for himself as well, may that be eradicated.

263
00:37:06,587 --> 00:37:08,869
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the division of compassion.

264
00:37:09,570 --> 00:37:10,030
[SPEAKER_01]: So it is

265
00:37:10,891 --> 00:37:14,574
[SPEAKER_01]: not based on the way people behave and the way they treat us.

266
00:37:14,755 --> 00:37:18,338
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why we can include all, we can extend it to all beings.

267
00:37:19,519 --> 00:37:23,663
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, therefore, the second one is this boundless compassion.

268
00:37:25,084 --> 00:37:30,269
[SPEAKER_01]: The third one is the boundless rejoicing.

269
00:37:31,190 --> 00:37:34,533
[SPEAKER_01]: It is a very altruistic act, in fact, to rejoice.

270
00:37:35,596 --> 00:37:52,086
[SPEAKER_01]: Like if you rejoice in the wonderful activities of someone in the past, like Gandhi, or anyone who has had a tremendous benefit to certain beings, to others, this is pure joy of appreciation.

271
00:37:52,867 --> 00:37:54,548
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing you can gain in return.

272
00:37:55,528 --> 00:37:57,730
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody is going to blame you if you don't do it.

273
00:37:57,830 --> 00:38:02,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody is going to praise you if you rejoice in someone's good deeds.

274
00:38:03,700 --> 00:38:12,663
[SPEAKER_01]: It's really a full appreciation that makes you in a way share the quality of what that person has done.

275
00:38:13,523 --> 00:38:20,746
[SPEAKER_01]: And also it is something that gives us an encouragement instead of being depressed because there's so much suffering everywhere.

276
00:38:20,786 --> 00:38:23,827
[SPEAKER_01]: There are good things also in which we can rejoice.

277
00:38:25,007 --> 00:38:28,008
[SPEAKER_01]: So the fourth one is impartiality.

278
00:38:28,288 --> 00:38:32,810
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's just to come back about what I was just saying that

279
00:38:33,967 --> 00:38:46,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Those three, loving-kindness, compassion and rejoicing, cannot be partial, cannot just be for those who we feel very close to.

280
00:38:48,498 --> 00:39:02,049
[SPEAKER_01]: It has to be extended to all, to strangers, to those difficult cases, for the very reason that I've mentioned, is that the purpose of these is to bring happiness and remove suffering,

281
00:39:02,884 --> 00:39:09,970
[SPEAKER_01]: And in whichever form that suffering might be, including the causes of suffering like hatred and so forth.

282
00:39:10,490 --> 00:39:16,735
[SPEAKER_01]: So anyway, those apply to every single being, no matter what they do.

283
00:39:18,717 --> 00:39:23,961
[SPEAKER_01]: And that impartiality is the only way that it can be really extended and universal.

284
00:39:25,182 --> 00:39:27,324
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's not just a vague sort of

285
00:39:28,695 --> 00:39:57,380
[SPEAKER_01]: deluded thing that has this sort of disembodied because of course it takes all sentient beings it's hard to represent ourselves all sentient beings but it is also something that creates a readiness that we can apply to any being that comes in the focus of our attention that means that we encounter in life and then because of this vast attitude

286
00:39:58,217 --> 00:40:04,540
[SPEAKER_01]: then we'll be ready to embrace that sentient being and not say, oh, this one is not from my group.

287
00:40:05,641 --> 00:40:07,482
[SPEAKER_01]: He's someone else.

288
00:40:08,442 --> 00:40:09,723
[SPEAKER_01]: He belongs to another.

289
00:40:10,323 --> 00:40:12,864
[SPEAKER_01]: So this is not my world.

290
00:40:14,165 --> 00:40:16,306
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not my job to take care of that person.

291
00:40:17,086 --> 00:40:24,730
[SPEAKER_01]: And so that will happen if you have only a very biased and partial loving kindness or compassion.

292
00:40:25,562 --> 00:40:38,913
[SPEAKER_01]: But once you have extended it to this meditation, then no matter what that person or that being is, when it comes into your attention, that being is suffering, you will wish to remove it.

293
00:40:39,814 --> 00:40:44,457
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you just see that being not particularly suffering, you will wish happiness.

294
00:40:45,438 --> 00:40:52,184
[SPEAKER_01]: So those are very crucial four points, because that's on the basis of that,

295
00:40:52,794 --> 00:41:14,265
[SPEAKER_01]: that we get a truly benevolent mind and that truly benevolent mind will then lead to the bodhicitta that is the wish may all beings may I gain the capacity to free all beings from suffering and bring them to enlightenment why that leads to bodhicitta?

296
00:41:14,345 --> 00:41:21,329
[SPEAKER_01]: because it makes us appreciate the fact that right now we don't have such capacity so we need to develop it

297
00:41:22,272 --> 00:41:27,995
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, we cannot practice this fully, this loving-kindness, compassion, and so forth.

298
00:41:28,955 --> 00:41:36,518
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, since we have, before we do the little break, we have a 20 minutes meditation.

299
00:41:37,359 --> 00:41:42,681
[SPEAKER_01]: So we'll try to, one by one, practice on those four.

300
00:41:43,602 --> 00:41:50,845
[SPEAKER_01]: And also, at the end, we'll say a few words how to do a permutation of those four

301
00:41:51,536 --> 00:41:55,898
[SPEAKER_01]: so as not to fall into possible deviations.

302
00:41:57,098 --> 00:42:02,801
[SPEAKER_01]: So let's begin with the first one, that is loving kindness.

303
00:42:04,141 --> 00:42:20,928
[SPEAKER_01]: So even though ultimately we want that to be again extended to all beings, as beginners we need to kindle that in our mind.

304
00:42:21,592 --> 00:42:23,753
[SPEAKER_01]: you need to bring that alive.

305
00:42:24,954 --> 00:42:47,268
[SPEAKER_01]: And so for that, it is really much easier to begin with someone for whom it is natural and very spontaneous and easy to let flow this loving-kindness, unconditional love, like it could be a child, it could be a companion, it could be a parent, a good friend,

306
00:42:47,814 --> 00:43:15,996
[SPEAKER_01]: animal whoever you feel like you don't have any difficulty to feel this unconditional love so although later we'll extend that more sort of towards all being as an unconditional feeling of love but in the beginning let's to in order to make that state of mind very vivid and very clear and intense in our mind let's first

307
00:43:16,718 --> 00:43:45,735
[SPEAKER_01]: visualize or imagine someone who is very dear to us or whoever you might want to choose but for the sake of having an image let's take the example that mostly people feel comfortable with it's like of a one of our child or a young relative and with a sort of beautiful innocent also vulnerable in a way small child

308
00:43:46,341 --> 00:44:09,972
[SPEAKER_01]: that comes towards us with like eyes trusting eyes with a big smile and then we sort of hug that child and there is nothing in us except like unconditional love that means may that child be happy may that child be safe may that child be healthy may that child progress and free

309
00:44:11,005 --> 00:44:13,025
[SPEAKER_01]: himself or herself from all suffering.

310
00:44:13,765 --> 00:44:19,707
[SPEAKER_01]: So this unconditional wish that made the child find the happiness and the cause of happiness.

311
00:44:20,607 --> 00:44:28,088
[SPEAKER_01]: And why do we so let's remember also that the causes of happiness is not just temporary comfort.

312
00:44:29,128 --> 00:44:33,529
[SPEAKER_01]: It's also finding a path to freedom.

313
00:44:34,549 --> 00:44:35,689
[SPEAKER_01]: It's finding wisdom.

314
00:44:36,229 --> 00:44:39,550
[SPEAKER_01]: It's finding the meaning in one's life.

315
00:44:40,412 --> 00:44:46,036
[SPEAKER_01]: and ultimately finding freedom from this cycle of existence.

316
00:44:47,156 --> 00:44:55,942
[SPEAKER_01]: And so deeper loving wishes than simply be well in the moment that will come.

317
00:44:57,022 --> 00:45:01,845
[SPEAKER_01]: So let's try to visualize that for a few moment.

318
00:45:02,846 --> 00:45:08,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, whoever you might choose as object of arousing loving kindness.

319
00:47:18,385 --> 00:47:27,691
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, when that feeling is quite vivid, let's make so that it really pervades the whole mental landscape.

320
00:47:27,991 --> 00:47:30,653
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing but loving kindness in our mind.

321
00:47:32,334 --> 00:47:41,961
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, thanks to that all-pervading feeling of loving kindness, now let's try to embrace other Satan beings as well.

322
00:47:43,141 --> 00:47:45,663
[SPEAKER_01]: Strangers and even difficult people.

323
00:47:46,768 --> 00:47:54,334
[SPEAKER_01]: and then limitless beings that we don't even, we cannot even represent ourselves.

324
00:47:54,374 --> 00:48:02,921
[SPEAKER_01]: So because this unconditioned love has no reason to be limited just to few, and it has the capacity to embrace all sentient beings.

325
00:48:05,063 --> 00:48:10,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Actually, it's even, we could almost say it's easier

326
00:48:12,118 --> 00:48:17,882
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the quality of this loving kindness is so much greater if it embraces all without exception.

327
00:48:18,823 --> 00:48:37,316
[SPEAKER_01]: And if we try to limit just a few beings, then it immediately loses in strength and quality, like a sun that will cease to shine upon everything, and suddenly it narrows and narrows and narrows, and just a few miserable beings that shine on a few plants or something.

328
00:48:37,877 --> 00:48:39,778
[SPEAKER_01]: So the sun will lose its sort of

329
00:48:40,590 --> 00:49:09,216
[SPEAKER_01]: brightness and magnificence so there's no need and actually it's diminishing to just try to reduce loving kindness so let it shine like a sun that shines equally on everything whether it's the sun shines equally on a beautiful place on heap of garbage on good people on people that commit harm the sun doesn't discriminate and so likewise this loving kindness should be in this way

330
00:51:14,550 --> 00:51:17,511
[SPEAKER_01]: Now the second one is the boundless compassion.

331
00:51:18,992 --> 00:51:29,455
[SPEAKER_01]: So in a way, it is the way loving kindness transforms when it encounters with suffering.

332
00:51:30,675 --> 00:51:36,197
[SPEAKER_01]: It stems from the same wish to do good, to be concerned by others.

333
00:51:37,998 --> 00:51:43,780
[SPEAKER_01]: But when it's suffering that it meets, then of course, it naturally becomes the wish may

334
00:51:44,499 --> 00:52:12,899
[SPEAKER_01]: suffering and its cause be alleviated so here the fact of mentioning the causes leads us much deeper that we might initially think because when the Buddha spoke about the truth of suffering he was not simply referring to obvious suffering

335
00:52:14,242 --> 00:52:41,528
[SPEAKER_01]: if you have a deep wound, if you have a strong toothache if we just lost a very very dear person it's obvious, obviously we know all its suffering in a way we don't need the Buddha to tell this is the truth of suffering but it's obvious to everyone so what why the Buddha was putting so much strength

336
00:52:42,327 --> 00:52:44,788
[SPEAKER_01]: on the need to recognize suffering.

337
00:52:45,748 --> 00:52:49,549
[SPEAKER_01]: It seems that we meet so much suffering that we don't need any teaching about that.

338
00:52:51,049 --> 00:53:01,851
[SPEAKER_01]: He was referring to something much more far-reaching than simply a toothache or a great sadness.

339
00:53:04,212 --> 00:53:10,373
[SPEAKER_01]: First of all, he was referring to the fact that what we usually consider as happiness

340
00:53:11,703 --> 00:53:41,135
[SPEAKER_01]: as already imbued with suffering because it will change like our youth our enjoyments particular circumstances those are subject to change very fast and something is very joyful reunion and a tragic event occurs or we take something that is very pleasant like a drug

341
00:53:42,199 --> 00:53:48,124
[SPEAKER_01]: And it seems that we're ecstatic, but there's so much suffering that will follow.

342
00:53:51,046 --> 00:54:08,901
[SPEAKER_01]: And more even than that, he was referring to the fact that something that we have hard time to even conceive, and we sometimes miss altogether, is that as long as we have a distorted view of reality,

343
00:54:10,075 --> 00:54:39,530
[SPEAKER_01]: that we take for permanent what is not that we believe that there is a separate autonomous self within ourselves when it's just a stream a dynamic stream of experience of consciousness when we make all those basic misconceptions about nature of reality that is not just an intellectual mistake it is a mistake or ignorance that will have

344
00:54:40,480 --> 00:55:08,212
[SPEAKER_01]: dramatic consequences because it leads to suffering again and again and again if you grasp the thing as solid and existing attraction and repulsion will take place then that will follow by hatred and craving and jealousy and arrogance and all those poisons that create suffering so that's really what the Buddha was saying about that's what we need to recognize

345
00:55:09,265 --> 00:55:28,742
[SPEAKER_01]: And that extends our compassion from an emotional compassion to the immediate suffering that can be very, very moving to a cognitive suffering that is linked with wisdom and a compassion that is based on understanding the root cause of suffering and wishing that the beings may be free from those.

346
00:55:30,364 --> 00:55:34,147
[SPEAKER_01]: But for the first one, at least the obvious suffering,

347
00:55:35,074 --> 00:55:41,599
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the easiest way to generate compassion as we generated loving kindness earlier.

348
00:55:43,120 --> 00:55:50,546
[SPEAKER_01]: So then we need for that to think of the unbearable suffering that are affecting so many sentient beings.

349
00:55:53,048 --> 00:56:03,236
[SPEAKER_01]: Mothers that die while giving birth, though it was supposed to be one of the happiest moments of their lives,

350
00:56:04,678 --> 00:56:21,387
[SPEAKER_01]: elderly people who suffer terribly from the degenerations of their faculties, their body, their organs that one by one give way, persecutions, massacres.

351
00:56:23,669 --> 00:56:29,772
[SPEAKER_01]: We can see so much of this in the images that the media deliver to us day after day.

352
00:56:31,727 --> 00:56:58,511
[SPEAKER_01]: of children in the refugee camp looking desperately for their mothers, mothers looking desperately for their children the suffering of separation with dear ones, the suffering of being confronted with those who do harm to us the mental suffering of being held in contempt

353
00:56:59,367 --> 00:57:25,452
[SPEAKER_01]: of being harmed by harsh words and the suffering of all satan beings of all kinds day after day millions and millions of animals are killed for our consumption some of them die in traps some of them die asphyxiated in nets some of them are stunned by electric

354
00:57:26,271 --> 00:57:52,885
[SPEAKER_01]: some of them are decapitated, this is endless stream of like ocean of blood and it's only the little bit one can see the great master Nagarjuna said that if we could pile up if we look from the Buddhist perspective of all past lives just the number of bodies that we had and suffered in as just being small ants

355
00:57:54,124 --> 00:58:02,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Just that, it will make a heap bigger than Mount Meru, the big mountain that's said to be at the center of the universe.

356
00:58:03,608 --> 00:58:14,113
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's to say, that if we are to generate compassion, there's certainly an unlimited store of objects of compassion.

357
00:58:15,294 --> 00:58:17,895
[SPEAKER_01]: So let's try to visualize some of those.

358
00:58:18,956 --> 00:58:21,357
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just to fall into some kind of distress,

359
00:58:23,400 --> 00:58:28,104
[SPEAKER_01]: and sort of empathic distress that makes us feel powerless and discouraged.

360
00:58:29,025 --> 00:58:36,571
[SPEAKER_01]: But rather, as His Holiness the Dalai Lama says, to generate the compassionate courage that comes with compassion.

361
00:58:37,172 --> 00:58:47,420
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if we relate the suffering of others to the impact they have on us, I feel so distressed, I cannot bear to see those images, then it's not compassion.

362
00:58:48,101 --> 00:58:50,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Compassion is like the doctor on the battlefield.

363
00:58:51,620 --> 00:58:59,425
[SPEAKER_01]: He sees so much suffering, but he's one after the other one trying to address and cure and take care of the wounded people.

364
00:58:59,445 --> 00:59:03,427
[SPEAKER_01]: He's not going to cry and cry each time he sees someone who is suffering.

365
00:59:04,968 --> 00:59:20,198
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if we react to the other suffering with compassion, not with just empathic distress, then, as His Holiness said, that the part of the Bodhisattva does the courage of compassion and not the

366
00:59:20,793 --> 00:59:26,345
[SPEAKER_01]: discouragement of feeling upset because of the suffering of others.

367
00:59:26,405 --> 00:59:29,953
[SPEAKER_01]: So let's try to do that for a few moments.

368
01:02:09,736 --> 01:02:38,201
[SPEAKER_01]: now the third one is rejoicing because of course there are not only terrible things in this world there are also wonderful accomplishments they can be relative accomplishments of people succeeding in their endeavor of which provided is of course it's a positive and constructive it's a virtuous endeavor people manifesting extraordinary qualities of

369
01:02:39,433 --> 01:02:49,021
[SPEAKER_01]: being wise, intelligent, compassionate, dedicating themselves to others, or achieving great goals that free many beings from suffering.

370
01:02:49,862 --> 01:03:06,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, take the example of Gandhi and Martin Luther King, Nelson Mandela, and besides all the great sages of the past, and those who created major changes in the world for good.

371
01:03:07,796 --> 01:03:08,417
[SPEAKER_01]: And simply,

372
01:03:09,038 --> 01:03:37,482
[SPEAKER_01]: around ourselves people who do simple act of goodness of generosity of kindness people who benefit others to sharing their knowledge and their wisdom and teaching others and people who help those in need people who give moral support to those who are in distress and actually our daily life is much more filled

373
01:03:38,414 --> 01:04:06,673
[SPEAKER_01]: with those act of kindnesses that nobody notices that doesn't make the first page of the newspaper or the evening news as a atrocious crime will immediately do but that's more what makes the texture of our daily life than acts of violence and and barbary and so forth so we should really truly appreciate that

374
01:04:07,257 --> 01:04:08,318
[SPEAKER_01]: Rejoice in that.

375
01:04:09,379 --> 01:04:18,246
[SPEAKER_01]: Wish that if somebody also finds happiness, may that person keep that happiness, may that happiness increase further.

376
01:04:19,247 --> 01:04:28,915
[SPEAKER_01]: If that person has some special qualities that are beneficial for that person and for others, may those qualities never decline but increase further.

377
01:04:30,016 --> 01:04:33,719
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, in short, it's rejoicing in others' qualities

378
01:04:34,648 --> 01:04:46,035
[SPEAKER_01]: wishing that those qualities of achievement or happiness never decline, but continue to increase and flourish further.

379
01:04:47,615 --> 01:04:53,339
[SPEAKER_01]: And do so without sort of... Rejoicing is the opposite of envy.

380
01:04:54,259 --> 01:04:59,903
[SPEAKER_01]: It means feeling uncomfortable because other people have qualities and other people are happy and so forth.

381
01:04:59,923 --> 01:05:03,485
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's like entirely accepting and rejoicing

382
01:05:04,817 --> 01:05:08,898
[SPEAKER_01]: others, whatever, goodness, enjoyments, qualities, and so forth.

383
01:05:19,241 --> 01:05:22,142
[SPEAKER_01]: It also includes gratitude, appreciation,

384
01:07:01,974 --> 01:07:07,637
[SPEAKER_01]: Now the fourth and last of these four immeasurables is impartiality.

385
01:07:07,738 --> 01:07:10,979
[SPEAKER_01]: So we already explained a little bit what it is.

386
01:07:13,081 --> 01:07:17,663
[SPEAKER_01]: It is to gradually extend the three former ones to all.

387
01:07:19,224 --> 01:07:31,632
[SPEAKER_01]: Realizing that once again this is not we are not casting moral judgment about those who deserve or not deserve our compassion or loving kindness.

388
01:07:33,593 --> 01:07:44,476
[SPEAKER_01]: But that since our ultimate goal is that all beings find happiness, all beings be free from suffering, so we are actually going at the root of that.

389
01:07:45,117 --> 01:07:53,239
[SPEAKER_01]: So if there's any cause that brings suffering, whether it's the hatred of a dictator or something, that is the cause.

390
01:07:54,099 --> 01:07:58,661
[SPEAKER_01]: Hate is the enemy, not the person who has fallen prey to hate.

391
01:07:59,221 --> 01:08:02,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, there will be never any end

392
01:08:02,617 --> 01:08:03,717
[SPEAKER_01]: to the cycle of hate.

393
01:08:05,198 --> 01:08:18,263
[SPEAKER_01]: So Shantideva gives the example in the Bodhicaryavatara, if someone beats you with a stick maybe almost no one except a really silly person will get angry at the stick.

394
01:08:19,203 --> 01:08:23,125
[SPEAKER_01]: Because we immediately know that the stick has been set in motion by someone.

395
01:08:23,745 --> 01:08:25,106
[SPEAKER_01]: So we get angry at the person.

396
01:08:26,146 --> 01:08:28,187
[SPEAKER_01]: But here we stop one step too early.

397
01:08:29,265 --> 01:08:33,388
[SPEAKER_01]: We should not be angry at the person because the person has been motivated by hatred.

398
01:08:34,449 --> 01:08:46,857
[SPEAKER_01]: So it is the hatred which is like a sickness that momentarily affects the person that is the true enemy, not the person who is fallen prey to hatred.

399
01:08:49,775 --> 01:09:01,303
[SPEAKER_01]: The hatred doesn't define the person, it defines certain circumstances, certain moments, certain conditions in the long history of that person.

400
01:09:02,523 --> 01:09:11,950
[SPEAKER_01]: Like when you go to see a doctor because you have a sickness, you don't tell the doctor, doctor, I am the flu.

401
01:09:12,830 --> 01:09:14,491
[SPEAKER_01]: You tell doctor, I have the flu.

402
01:09:14,992 --> 01:09:18,454
[SPEAKER_01]: You make the distinction between your sickness and what you are.

403
01:09:19,450 --> 01:09:29,538
[SPEAKER_01]: So hatred can be like a long-term sickness for some, but it's still an aberration, a deviation, a sickness of the mind.

404
01:09:30,138 --> 01:09:31,399
[SPEAKER_01]: So it is that we should treat.

405
01:09:31,660 --> 01:09:45,130
[SPEAKER_01]: So with this in mind, we can impartially extend loving kindness and compassion without discrimination and without sort of restraint to all sentient beings.

406
01:09:45,631 --> 01:09:47,292
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's the fourth step.

407
01:10:51,902 --> 01:11:14,097
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, just to conclude, in one of the teachings that Khyentse Rinpoche would often give, the treasure of precious quality, or the Yonten Tzu in Tibetan, there is a way to combine those four, and especially a way that prevents one from falling into some kind of deviation.

408
01:11:14,878 --> 01:11:17,380
[SPEAKER_01]: There are possible deviations to each of those four.

409
01:11:18,200 --> 01:11:20,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Loving kindness can stray into attachment.

410
01:11:22,783 --> 01:11:41,092
[SPEAKER_01]: So if that happens then you should when that happens you should shift to impartiality because impartiality makes that we cannot be biased with attachment to someone in particular at the exclusion of others.

411
01:11:42,612 --> 01:11:52,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you shift to impartiality but if you continue to meditate on impartiality if it lacks sort of

412
01:11:53,372 --> 01:11:57,273
[SPEAKER_01]: clarity and wisdom, it may fall in indifference.

413
01:11:58,294 --> 01:12:06,296
[SPEAKER_01]: Instead of equally caring for everyone, you equally don't care for anyone, whether they are friends, enemies, or strangers.

414
01:12:07,117 --> 01:12:09,517
[SPEAKER_01]: So then you must wake up from that.

415
01:12:09,657 --> 01:12:15,799
[SPEAKER_01]: If that happens, if you sink in a kind of vague indifference, then you will shift to compassion.

416
01:12:15,900 --> 01:12:23,162
[SPEAKER_01]: Because compassion, the contemplation of intense suffering, cannot but wake up your mind

417
01:12:23,787 --> 01:12:50,118
[SPEAKER_01]: into a compassionate mind now if you keep meditating on compassion and do so again and again focusing on suffering it might be that while contemplating the immensity of suffering you might feel overwhelmed there's no way I could make any change there's so much suffering again and again so it's kind of falling into a kind of deep

418
01:12:51,403 --> 01:13:17,767
[SPEAKER_01]: discouragement that come from being overwhelmed so then is the time when that sort of deviation occurs instead of courage you feel discouraged to shift to rejoicing and because there are good things that lift up your spirits and so you can keep on meditating for a while on rejoicing now if rejoicing becomes sort of too like a

419
01:13:18,790 --> 01:13:32,881
[SPEAKER_01]: seeing everything, the whole world, through tinted glasses, like everything is rosy and nice, which is not quite the case, then you can go again to compassion, or to loving kindness, or to etc., etc.

420
01:13:33,462 --> 01:13:46,252
[SPEAKER_01]: So by permuting those four, they will, each other will enhance each other, plus we will avoid to fall into some kind of...

421
01:13:47,571 --> 01:14:17,177
[SPEAKER_01]: sort of stagnating into some kind of side track so that's in short the how we can practice these four immeasurable ideas or meditations and so I think we have planned to have a short break and then after that Rinpoche will speak of the absolute bodhicitta so maybe 10-15 minute breaks

